<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · ideology</p><h1 class="enc-title">Feminism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1792">1792 CE</a> – present</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Feminism is a range of movements and ideologies, originating in late 18th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, which hold that societies are patriarchal and seek the equality of the sexes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/feminism.jpg" alt="Feminism" width="330" height="233" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Feminist_Suffrage_Parade_in_New_York_City,_1912.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Feminist movements originated in late 18th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Mary Wollstonecraft is seen by many as a founder of feminism because of her book <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/a-vindication-of-the-rights-of-woman">A Vindication of the Rights of Woman</a> (1792), in which she argues that class and private property are the basis of discrimination against women, and that women as much as men needed equal rights.</p><p>The word came later. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-fourier">Charles Fourier</a> is credited with coining féminisme in 1837, but no trace of the word has been found in his works. It first appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1871, in a medical thesis by Ferdinand-Valère Faneau de la Cour, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandre-dumas">Alexandre Dumas</a> fils coined féministe in 1872 for men who supported women's rights; both uses were very negative. The Oxford English Dictionary dates its first appearance in English in this meaning to 1895.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Feminism is a range of movements and ideologies that aim to establish the political, economic, personal and social equality of the sexes. It holds that modern societies are patriarchal and that women are treated unjustly in them. Since the 19th century, first-wave liberal feminism, which sought political and legal equality through reforms within a liberal democratic framework, was contrasted with labour-based proletarian women's movements that developed into socialist and Marxist feminism. Since the 1960s both have been contrasted with radical feminism, which arose from the radical wing of second-wave feminism.</p><p>Feminist theory emerged from the movements and draws on anthropology, sociology, economics, literary criticism, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psychoanalysis">psychoanalysis</a> and philosophy to understand gender inequality. In the 1970s French feminists such as Hélène Cixous developed the concept of écriture féminine, or female writing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Many scholars consider feminist campaigns a main force behind changes in women's rights, particularly in the West, where they are near-universally credited with women's suffrage, reproductive rights and the right to enter into contracts and own property. At the International Conference of Socialist Women in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a> in 1907, suffrage was described as a tool of class struggle. In 1918 Constance Markievicz became the first woman elected to the British House of Commons, but in line with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinn-fein">Sinn Féin</a> policy she did not take her seat. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> the National Organization for Women began in 1966 to seek equality, including through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/equal-rights-amendment">Equal Rights Amendment</a>, which did not pass.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Feminism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…colours, such as the purple, white and green of the Women’s Social and Political Union, hunger strikes and deliberate law-breaking became part of the repertoire of later movements, and activists of the 1960s called their own campaigns a “second wave” of <b>feminism</b>, making the suffrage era the first.</blockquote><a href="/movement/womens-suffrage-movement">Women's suffrage movement · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>By the end of the Second World War the movement had been severely weakened, but it revived in the 1960s in the anti-nuclear, environmental and peace movements, the counterculture and the New Left, and later in punk, anarcha-<b>feminism</b> and black anarchism.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/anarchism">Anarchism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>Mary Wollstonecraft’s A Vindication of the Rights of Woman (1792) founded liberal <b>feminism</b>.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/liberalism">Liberalism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>Christian and Islamic socialism, guild socialism, syndicalism, socialist <b>feminism</b> and eco-socialism, which credits William Morris with its early principles, each took the ideas their own way.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/socialism">Socialism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li></ul></section>
